<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.2.1: Interpreting Graphs of Systems of Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00387</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.2.1: Interpreting Graphs of Systems of Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>766af223-a9ac-49c1-af47-88bab2959b18</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Warm Up Activity</title>
      <para>For questions 1 – 6, use the given graph and scenario:</para>
      <para>
        <quote>
          <figure class="unnumbered" id="fig-00001">
            <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_04_02_01.jpg" />
            </media>
          
        </quote>
        <quote
         >A club is selling snacks at a track meet. Oranges cost $1 each and
        protein bars cost $4 each. They sell a total of 100 items, and collect $304.</quote>
     </para>
      <!--Q#1-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What do you notice?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <list>
            <item>There are two lines.</item>
            <item>It’s about snacks.</item>
            <item>The lines intersect.</item>
            <item>The point of intersection is labeled \((32, 68)\).</item>
            <item>The sum of 32 and 68 is 100.</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What do you wonder?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para>Things students may wonder:</para>
          <list>
            <item>What does each line mean?</item>
            <item>What does the point of intersection mean?</item>
            <item>Where can you see the information from the problem on the graph?</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise id="_0e4967e0-47aa-4365-a3a3-273c127972be" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          
          <para>What is the approximate \(y\)–intercept of the green dashed line?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct! 76</para>
          <para>The correct answer is 76</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_06a7d7a7-7e8c-4da7-83bc-bc262305a054" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          
          <para>What is the approximate slope of the green dashed line?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct! \(-\frac14 \)</para>
          <para>The correct answer is \(-\frac14 \)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_072f94e5-fbe6-4d9a-a082-1f5a8a31e472" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>What is the approximate \(y\)-intercept of the orange solid line?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct! 100</para>
          <para>the correct answer is 100.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Q#6-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What is the approximate slope of the orange solid line?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. -1</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
    </section>
  </content>
